('G0001G','grc','α, Ἀλφα','Alpha','G:N-LI','Alpha','Α, α, ἄλφα (which see), τό, indecl., 
 alpha, the first letter of the Greek alphabet. As a numeral, ά = 1, α = 1000. As a prefix, it appears to have at least two and perhaps three distinct senses: 
__1. ἀ- (before a vowel, ἀν-) negative, as in ἄ-γνωστος, ἄ-δικος. 
 __2. ἀ-, ἁ- copulative, indicating community and fellowship, as in ἁ-πλοῦς, ἀ-κολουθέω, ἀ-δελφός. 
 __3. An intensive force (LS, s. α), as in ἀ-τενίζω is sometimes assumed (but see Boisacq, see word).
 (AS)'),
('G0002','grc','Ἀαρών','Aarōn','N:N-M-P','Aaron','Ἀαρών (Heb. אַהֲרוֹן), indecl. (in FlJ, -ῶνος), 
 Aaron (Exo.4:14, al.): Luk.1:5, Act.7:40, Heb.5:4, 7:11, 9:4.† 
 (AS)'),
('G0003','grc','Ἀβαδδών','Abaddōn','N:N--T','Abaddon','Ἀβαδδών (Heb. אֲבַדּוֹן, destruction; LXX, ἀπώλεια, only in Wisdom Lit., of the place of the ruined dead: Job.26:6, 28:22, 31:12, Psa.88:12, Pro.15:11*), indecl.; 
 in NT, Abaddon, the angel of the Abyss: Rev.9:11.†
 (AS)'),
